          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Escape</strong><br>\nHarriet Tubman was born Araminta Ross around 1822 in Maryland, enslaved from birth. She endured hardships including physical violence, which caused lifelong health issues. In 1849, Tubman escaped slavery, fleeing to Philadelphia.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Underground Railroad</strong><br>\nAfter her escape, Tubman became a conductor on the Underground Railroad, a network of safe houses for runaway slaves. She risked her life to lead enslaved people to freedom, guiding dozens across the Mason-Dixon Line.</p>\n\n<p><strong>During the Civil War</strong><br>\nTubman served as a scout, spy, and nurse for the Union Army during the Civil War. She supported the army's efforts to liberate slaves, using her knowledge of covert operations and the southern terrain.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
